speechtext:
If this amendment is adopted, it will change the whole course of common law in Canada, let us well understand that. Now, I have great respect for the legal ability of the hon. gentleman from St. John's and Iberville (Mr. Demers), whom I have had the pleasure of knowing intimately ; I have great respect for his judgment and legal knowledge, and would willingly support any measure that he brought forwai-d, were I not constrained to oppose it on strictly legal grounds. Now, with regard to our friends across the border, I have often noticed that they would pass a law today, the courts would discuss it, and in three or four years it would disappear from the statute-book, and they would go back to the sound principles of the common law, which they inherited from the old country. We ought not to depart from the common law of this country, unless for the most cogent and sound reasons. I have listened to the hon. member for Lincoln (Mr. Lancaster) with

great deference on many occasions, but I do not think that he puts this question today in a very strong light. He was talking about a trial in court. Why, we have the same difficulty in every case we try. Very often we cannot make out our case. I have had dozens of cases where I knew my client was right, but I was unable to prove my points, therefore the court had to rule against me. Would it be wise for me, because I failed in my evidence, to come to parliament and ask them to pass a law to enable me to get damages under circumstances where I had failed to meet the requirements of the law ? All that I understand to be alleged in favour of this amendment, is that it is difficult in some cases to make out your case against the railway company. Is this parliament going to adopt as a sound reason for legislation, that because a man fails to make out his case it must be made easier for him to do so ? There may be hardship, but hardship is the common argument that makes bad law. Whenever a man commences to talk about hardship, and desires parliament to legislate to remove the hardship, then he is inviting us to make a bad law. There is always hardship, and always will be, in the administration of any system of law. You never can get any principle of law that will fit every particular case. The common law of England says that damages must be awarded and paid, if the railway company does damage. But suppose you fail to show that the railway company has caused damage do you want this parliament to pronounce judgment against the railway company because there was a failure of evidence ? But I go further. I was a student of civil law at one time, and I say, with all deference to those gentlemen who are practising the civil law, that I will not defer to them in the knowledge of the principles of civil law. I undertake to say, after reading the judgment of the Privy Council, that it is a correct exposition of the civil law of Rome, as it is of the common law of this country. Therefore, these hon. gentlemen from Quebec are asking us not only to repeal the civil law, but also the common law of this country. I say that is a position this parliament ought not to take. The whole point is this : We charter a railway company, or any other company, and give it the necessary rights to carry on its business. Now, remember I am talking to lawyers and men of common sense. I don't want to hear any layman get up in this parliament and differentiate himself from a lawyer, when we are talking about common sense. The law is the essence of common sense, and ^man who has common sense and who has good judgment, is always a good lawyer. I want this parliament to understand that when we charter a railway company to run a railway, we charter it with the implied agreement that it will run that railway without do-198}
ing wrong to any individual. They are bound to build the railway and to run it according to the latest improvements, with the most correct appliances. If we pass a law saying they shall be liable for damages, without proof of negligence we are acting without common sense and without common honesty, and that is what is sought to be done here. We charter a railway company, the company take our charter and build their line, use the best engines and cars, then when an accident happens, if they can show that they have done all that the law requires them to do, the law says they are not liable, because there is no negligence.
